Both commands are used to check running processes, but they work differently.

Simple difference

ps = Take a snapshot 📸

top = Watch continuously 📹

1. ps

ps shows the processes at the moment you run the command.

ps -ef

=> Example:

root       1     0  0  ... /sbin/init
root     700     1  0  ... sshd
bilal   1234  1200  0  ... bash

If you want to find a particular process:

ps -ef | grep ssh

Good for:

Finding a process
Getting the PID
Checking whether a service/process is running
Using in scripts

2. top

Run:

top

You'll get a continuously updating screen:

PID   USER    %CPU   %MEM   COMMAND
1234  bilal    5.2    2.1   python
700   root     1.0    0.5   sshd
1     root     0.0    0.1   systemd

top keeps refreshing automatically.

You can see:

CPU usage
Memory usage
Running processes
Load average
Number of processes
Which processes are consuming the most resources.

Press:

q

to exit top.

=> Main difference

| Feature                    | `ps`             | `top`                    |
| -------------------------- | ---------------- | ------------------------ |
| Shows processes            | ✅                | ✅                        |
| One-time snapshot          | ✅                | ❌                        |
| Live/continuous monitoring | ❌                | ✅                        |
| CPU usage                  | ✅                | ✅                        |
| Memory usage               | ✅                | ✅                        |
| Find specific process      | Easy with `grep` | Can search interactively |
| Good for scripts           | ✅                | Usually not              |
| Resource troubleshooting   | Basic            | ✅ Excellent              |

=> Real Linux admin example

Suppose a user says:

"The server is very slow."

First, you might run:

top

and watch which process is consuming high CPU or memory.

You find:

PID    %CPU    %MEM    COMMAND
2456   95.0    10.2    java

Then you can get more details:

ps -fp 2456

So they can be used together:

top → Find the problematic process
       ↓
ps  → Get detailed information about that PID
